WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.178
CNC means that a computer drives the machine’s motors, following a program.

2
00:00:06.728 --> 00:00:08.504
This is a machining centre.

3
00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:12.235
The spindle is the turning shaft that holds the tool.

4
00:00:12.786 --> 00:00:16.153
Right now it holds a probe: a measuring stylus.

5
00:00:17.053 --> 00:00:22.024
The probe touches the fixed jaw of the vice, and then the top of the plate.

6
00:00:22.924 --> 00:00:26.247
From that comes the work offset, G54.

7
00:00:26.797 --> 00:00:29.885
It tells the machine where the part sits on the table.

8
00:00:30.435 --> 00:00:33.523
Every position in the program counts from that point.

9
00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:38.403
Every tool sticks out of the spindle by a different amount.

10
00:00:38.953 --> 00:00:43.376
So the tool offset of each tool stores its length and its radius.

11
00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:47.588
Tool 5, a drill, touches the tool setter.

12
00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:53.957
Its length, 121.43 millimetres, goes into the table.

13
00:00:54.507 --> 00:00:57.468
Together those numbers are called the offsets.

14
00:00:58.368 --> 00:01:02.548
The program itself is G-code: a list of short commands.

15
00:01:03.098 --> 00:01:10.440
G00 is a rapid move, G01 a straight cut, M03 starts the spindle.

16
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:15.251
Usually CAM software writes that code from the 3D model.

17
00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:18.216
After every changeover,

18
00:01:18.416 --> 00:01:23.327
the first part is measured in full before the run starts: the first-off check.

19
00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:25.015
Is the slot right?

20
00:01:25.565 --> 00:01:27.121
Are the holes at depth?

21
00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.355
That takes 10 to 20 minutes.

22
00:01:30.905 --> 00:01:34.562
But one wrong number repeats on every part:

23
00:01:34.562 --> 00:01:38.730
200 parts times 35 euros is 7,000 euros of scrap.

24
00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.580
Only then does the run start.

25
00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:46.205
A program that has already run without problems is called proven.

26
00:01:47.405 --> 00:01:52.051
The control reads the program from top to bottom, one line at a time.

27
00:01:52.602 --> 00:01:56.354
Each block is one step, with a number such as N10.

28
00:01:57.254 --> 00:01:59.146
An M-code is a switch.

29
00:01:59.696 --> 00:02:08.035
M06 changes to tool 3, M03 starts the spindle at 3,200 revolutions a minute,

30
00:02:08.235 --> 00:02:10.081
M08 the coolant.

31
00:02:10.981 --> 00:02:19.285
G90 switches on absolute programming: X0, Y minus 10 counts from the zero point G54.

32
00:02:20.185 --> 00:02:26.341
With G91, incremental programming, a position counts from where the tool is now.

33
00:02:26.891 --> 00:02:29.445
Mixing up the two is a classic crash.

34
00:02:30.345 --> 00:02:35.558
G01 is modal: it stays active until another code replaces it.

35
00:02:36.108 --> 00:02:44.043
Block N70 only says Y40, and still cuts straight, at 640 millimetres per minute.

36
00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:48.578
The program describes the part, not the tool.

37
00:02:49.128 --> 00:02:55.734
G43 H3 switches on tool length compensation: the length of tool 3 is added in.

38
00:02:56.634 --> 00:03:03.022
With G41, cutter radius compensation, the path shifts sideways by one radius.

39
00:03:03.572 --> 00:03:09.206
If a new cutter measures 9.98, the setter enters 4.99,

40
00:03:09.406 --> 00:03:11.844
and the same program is still right.

41
00:03:12.744 --> 00:03:16.239
If a bore measures 0.01 undersize,

42
00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:21.884
the operator enters a wear offset: 0.005 on the radius,

43
00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:25.579
half, because a radius counts twice in a diameter.

44
00:03:26.779 --> 00:03:27.998
Now it goes wrong.

45
00:03:28.548 --> 00:03:36.262
For the drill, the setter types 112.43, instead of 121.43.

46
00:03:37.162 --> 00:03:40.668
The control thinks the drill is 9 millimetres shorter,

47
00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:44.490
and sends it 9 millimetres deeper: right through the floor.

48
00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.411
If it hits the vice, that is a crash.

49
00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:53.108
Of 6 crashes in a year, 3 came from a typed length,

50
00:03:53.309 --> 00:03:57.024
2 from a wrong H number and 1 from the zero point.

51
00:03:57.574 --> 00:04:00.384
The program was right; the table was not.

52
00:04:01.584 --> 00:04:06.634
CAM software turns the 3D model into a toolpath: the route of the tool,

53
00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:08.715
with feeds and speeds.

54
00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:14.456
A post-processor translates that path into the G-code of this one machine.

55
00:04:15.006 --> 00:04:17.165
Two machines, two programs.

56
00:04:18.065 --> 00:04:22.117
The simulation runs that code on a digital copy of the machine,

57
00:04:22.317 --> 00:04:25.173
and catches the collision with the jaw first.

58
00:04:26.073 --> 00:04:30.127
Then the prove-out: the first real run, block by block,

59
00:04:30.328 --> 00:04:32.337
until the first part is good.

60
00:04:32.886 --> 00:04:37.718
At 150 programs a year, every hour of it counts.
